{"assumption_ids": [], "boundary": "A result as the authors number it. Its links to the Lean were made with AI for Proof Reader and have not been reviewed by the authors.", "citation_ids": ["cite.euler.lemma-2.1"], "evidence_ids": ["link.euler.lemma-2.1"], "id": "euler.lemma-2.1", "kind": "claim", "label": "Lemma 2.1", "locator": "Lemma 2.1, Section 2", "source_id": "paper", "statement": "**Lemma 2.1.** Let the wave vector $k(t)\ne0$ and the amplitude vector $\mathbf a(t)$, functions of time alone, satisfy $$\begin{equation}
 k'=-D^Tk,\qquad
 \mathbf a'=-D\mathbf a+p_{\rm w} k,\qquad
 p_{\rm w}=\frac{2k\cdot D\mathbf a}{|k|^2},\qquad
 k\cdot\mathbf a=0. \tag{2.1}
\end{equation}$$ Then $Dx+\mathbf aF(k\cdot x)$ is divergence free and solves the Euler equations on $\mathbb R^3$ with pressure $-x\cdot(D'+D^2)x/2-p_{\rm w} P(k\cdot x)$. If $k(t_0)\ne0$, $\mathbf a(t_0)\ne0$ and $k(t_0)\cdot\mathbf a(t_0)=0$, then both vectors remain nonzero and perpendicular for every $t\ge t_0$ in the interval of existence of $D$.", "status": "unverified"}

{"assumption_ids": [], "boundary": "A result as the authors number it. Its links to the Lean were made with AI for Proof Reader and have not been reviewed by the authors.", "citation_ids": ["cite.euler.lemma-2.2"], "evidence_ids": ["link.euler.lemma-2.2"], "id": "euler.lemma-2.2", "kind": "claim", "label": "Lemma 2.2", "locator": "Lemma 2.2, Section 2", "source_id": "paper", "statement": "**Lemma 2.2.** Let $B(t)$ be a smooth trace-free matrix, and let $k\ne0$ and $\mathbf a\ne0$ satisfy (2.1) with $D$ replaced by $B$. Put $E_1=\frac{k}{|k|}$, $E_2=\frac{\mathbf a}{|\mathbf a|}$ and $E_3=E_1\times E_2$, write $B_{ij}=E_i\cdot BE_j$ $(i,j=1,2,3)$ for the entries of $B$ in this orthonormal frame, and let $\sigma=|k|\,|\mathbf a|$, $\nu=B_{12}$ and $\eta=B_{32}$. Then $$\begin{equation}
 E_1'=-\nu E_2-B_{13}E_3,\quad
 E_2'=\nu E_1-\eta E_3,\quad
 E_3'=B_{13}E_1+\eta E_2,
 \qquad(\log\sigma)'=B_{33}. \tag{2.2}
\end{equation}$$", "status": "unverified"}

{"assumption_ids": [], "boundary": "A result as the authors number it. Its links to the Lean were made with AI for Proof Reader and have not been reviewed by the authors.", "citation_ids": ["cite.euler.lemma-2.3"], "evidence_ids": ["link.euler.lemma-2.3"], "id": "euler.lemma-2.3", "kind": "claim", "label": "Lemma 2.3", "locator": "Lemma 2.3, Section 2", "source_id": "paper", "statement": "**Lemma 2.3.** Suppose that on $0\le\tau\le T$ the entries of $B$ in the frame are $B_{12}=\nu$, $B_{32}=\eta$ and $B_{ij}=0$ otherwise, and that $E_c=0$. Then $\sigma_p=\sigma$, and the scaled components of the wave vector satisfy $$\zeta_1'=-\Bigl(1+\frac1{2S^2}\Bigr)\zeta_2,\qquad
 \zeta_2'=-\varepsilon\zeta_3,\qquad
 \zeta_3'=\frac{\varepsilon}{2S^2}\zeta_2 .$$ Where $\zeta_3\ne0$, the components $\alpha_1,\alpha_2$ of every amplitude vector satisfy $$\begin{aligned}
 \alpha_1'&=-\alpha_2+\zeta_1\Pi,\\
 \alpha_2'&=-\Bigl(1-\frac1{2S^2}\Bigr)\alpha_1
   +\frac{\varepsilon}{2S^2}\,\frac{\zeta_1\alpha_1+\zeta_2\alpha_2}{\zeta_3}
   +\frac{\zeta_2}{S^2}\,\Pi,
 \end{aligned}
 \qquad
 \Pi=\frac{2\zeta_2\alpha_1+(\zeta_1+\varepsilon\zeta_3)\alpha_2}m .$$ As $S\to\infty$ with $\varepsilon$ and $T$ fixed, $\zeta\to\zeta_0$ uniformly on $[0,T]$, and the coefficients of this system for $(\alpha_1,\alpha_2)$ converge uniformly on $[0,T]$ to those of $$\begin{equation}
 \alpha_1'=-\cos^2\varphi(1-\varepsilon\tan\varphi)\alpha_2
                 -\varepsilon\tau\sin2\varphi\,\alpha_1,
 \qquad \alpha_2'=-\alpha_1 . \tag{2.5}
\end{equation}$$", "status": "unverified"}

{"assumption_ids": [], "boundary": "A result as the authors number it. Its links to the Lean were made with AI for Proof Reader and have not been reviewed by the authors.", "citation_ids": ["cite.euler.lemma-2.4"], "evidence_ids": ["link.euler.lemma-2.4"], "id": "euler.lemma-2.4", "kind": "claim", "label": "Lemma 2.4", "locator": "Lemma 2.4, Section 2", "source_id": "paper", "statement": "**Lemma 2.4.** Assume (2.7). The scaled components of the wave vector satisfy $$\begin{equation}
 \sup|\zeta-\zeta_0|\le CT^C\delta\le\tfrac12,\qquad
 \sup\left|\frac{|k|^2\cos^2\varphi}{\lambda^2}-1\right|
       \le CT^C\delta\le\tfrac12. \tag{2.10}
\end{equation}$$ In particular $$\begin{equation}
 \tfrac12\le\zeta_3\le\tfrac32,\qquad |\zeta|\le3T^2 . \tag{2.11}
\end{equation}$$ The components $\alpha_1,\alpha_2$ of every amplitude vector satisfy $$\frac{d}{d\tau}\binom{\alpha_1}{\alpha_2}
 =\left\{-\begin{pmatrix}
 \varepsilon\tau\sin2\varphi&\cos^2\varphi(1-\varepsilon\tan\varphi)\\
 1&0
 \end{pmatrix}+\mathcal E(\tau)\right\}\binom{\alpha_1}{\alpha_2},
 \qquad \sup|\mathcal E|\le CT^C\delta,$$ where $\mathcal E$ is the same for all amplitude vectors.", "status": "unverified"}

{"assumption_ids": [], "boundary": "A result as the authors number it. Its links to the Lean were made with AI for Proof Reader and have not been reviewed by the authors.", "citation_ids": ["cite.euler.lemma-2.5"], "evidence_ids": ["link.euler.lemma-2.5"], "id": "euler.lemma-2.5", "kind": "claim", "label": "Lemma 2.5", "locator": "Lemma 2.5, Section 2", "source_id": "paper", "statement": "**Lemma 2.5.** Let $0<\varepsilon\le1$. The solution $Y$ of (2.12) exists for all $\tau\ge0$, satisfies $|Y|\le1$, and has the form $$\begin{equation}
 Y=(1+e)\cos\varphi-\tfrac14\varepsilon\tau\sin2\varphi,
 \qquad 0\le e\le\tfrac18\varepsilon. \tag{2.13}
\end{equation}$$ The reference amplitude satisfies $$\begin{equation}
 a_{\rm ref}(\tau)=\sqrt{\cos\varphi(\tau)}
       \exp\left(\int_0^\tau(1+e)\cos\varphi\,d\tau'\right). \tag{2.14}
\end{equation}$$ The function $Y$ has exactly one zero $\tau_m$, with $2\le\varepsilon\tau_m\le3$. It is positive before this zero and negative afterwards, so $a_{\rm ref}$ is largest at $\tau_m$, and $$\log a_{\rm ref}(\tau_m)\ge\varepsilon^{-1/2}-1.$$", "status": "unverified"}

{"assumption_ids": [], "boundary": "A result as the authors number it. Its links to the Lean were made with AI for Proof Reader and have not been reviewed by the authors.", "citation_ids": ["cite.euler.lemma-2.6"], "evidence_ids": ["link.euler.lemma-2.6"], "id": "euler.lemma-2.6", "kind": "claim", "label": "Lemma 2.6", "locator": "Lemma 2.6, Section 2", "source_id": "paper", "statement": "**Lemma 2.6.** Assume (2.7), and let $\mathbf a$ be the amplitude vector with $\mathbf a(t_0)=-E_1(t_0)/S+E_2(t_0)$, that is, with $(\alpha_1,\alpha_2)(0)=(-1,1)$ in (2.3), the initial data of the reference solution. Then $\alpha_2>0$ on $0\le\tau\le T$, and $$\begin{equation}
 \left|\frac{\alpha_1}{\alpha_2}+Y\right|\le CT^C\delta,\qquad
 \alpha_2\le|\mathbf a|\le(1+CT^C\delta)\alpha_2. \tag{2.17}
\end{equation}$$ Moreover, $$\begin{equation}
 \left|\log\frac{|\mathbf a|}{a_{\rm ref}}\right|\le\varepsilon. \tag{2.18}
\end{equation}$$ For $0\le\tau_1\le\tau_2\le T$, let $\Phi(\tau_2,\tau_1)$ map $\mathbf b(\tau_1)$ to $\mathbf b(\tau_2)$ for every amplitude vector $\mathbf b$. It maps the plane perpendicular to $k(\tau_1)$ onto the plane perpendicular to $k(\tau_2)$, and $$\|\Phi(\tau_2,\tau_1)\|
 \le CS\,\frac{|\mathbf a(\tau_2)|}{|\mathbf a(\tau_1)|}.$$", "status": "unverified"}

{"assumption_ids": [], "boundary": "A result as the authors number it. Its links to the Lean were made with AI for Proof Reader and have not been reviewed by the authors.", "citation_ids": ["cite.euler.lemma-2.7"], "evidence_ids": ["link.euler.lemma-2.7"], "id": "euler.lemma-2.7", "kind": "claim", "label": "Lemma 2.7", "locator": "Lemma 2.7, Section 2", "source_id": "paper", "statement": "**Lemma 2.7.** Assume (2.7), and let $\mathbf a$ be the amplitude vector of Lemma 2.6. Put $E_{1,+}=k/|k|$, $E_{2,+}=\mathbf a/|\mathbf a|$ and $E_{3,+}=E_{1,+}\times E_{2,+}$, and define $$\nu_+=E_{1,+}\cdot ME_{2,+},\qquad
 \eta_+=E_{3,+}\cdot ME_{2,+}.$$ Then $\nu_+\ge\varepsilon\nu/2$ on $0\le\tau\le T$. Where $\varepsilon\tan\varphi\ge1$, that is, for $\sqrt2/\varepsilon\le\tau\le T$, $$\begin{equation}
 \left|\frac{\nu_+}{\nu}-1\right|\le\varepsilon,\qquad
 \left|\frac{\eta_+/\nu_+}{\cos\varphi}-1\right|\le\frac12. \tag{2.19}
\end{equation}$$", "status": "unverified"}

{"assumption_ids": [], "boundary": "A result as the authors number it. Its links to the Lean were made with AI for Proof Reader and have not been reviewed by the authors.", "citation_ids": ["cite.euler.lemma-4.1"], "evidence_ids": ["link.euler.lemma-4.1"], "id": "euler.lemma-4.1", "kind": "claim", "label": "Lemma 4.1", "locator": "Lemma 4.1, Section 4", "source_id": "paper", "statement": "**Lemma 4.1.** Assume $H^\#\le\operatorname{Id}$ on $I\times\mathbb T^3$ as quadratic forms. Then for every $a\in\mathbb T^3$, $z\in H^1_0(I;\mathbb R^3)$ and $\xi=D_aX(\cdot,a)z$, one has $\int_I H^\#\xi\cdot\xi\,\mathrm dt\le\frac12\int_I|\xi_t|^2\,\mathrm dt$. Hence $$\begin{equation}
 \int_I(Kz_t\cdot z_t+\Omega z_t\cdot z)\,\mathrm dt
 \ge\frac12\int_I|\xi_t|^2\,\mathrm dt. \tag{4.8}
\end{equation}$$", "status": "unverified"}

{"assumption_ids": [], "boundary": "A result as the authors number it. Its links to the Lean were made with AI for Proof Reader and have not been reviewed by the authors.", "citation_ids": ["cite.euler.lemma-5.3"], "evidence_ids": ["link.euler.lemma-5.3"], "id": "euler.lemma-5.3", "kind": "claim", "label": "Lemma 5.3", "locator": "Lemma 5.3, Section 5", "source_id": "paper", "statement": "**Lemma 5.3.** For $t_0\le t\le T$ and $|a|\le2\ell_*$, the seed satisfies $$\begin{equation}
 |\mathbf a(t,a)|\le2|\mathbf a(t,0)|,\qquad
 (\kappa\cdot M\mathbf a)(t,a)>0 . \tag{5.19}
\end{equation}$$ There is an absolute exponent $C$ such that the following holds at every order $n\ge0$.", "status": "unverified"}

{"assumption_ids": [], "boundary": "A result as the authors number it. Its links to the Lean were made with AI for Proof Reader and have not been reviewed by the authors.", "citation_ids": ["cite.euler.lemma-5.5"], "evidence_ids": ["link.euler.lemma-5.5"], "id": "euler.lemma-5.5", "kind": "claim", "label": "Lemma 5.5", "locator": "Lemma 5.5, Section 5", "source_id": "paper", "statement": "**Lemma 5.5.** There is an absolute exponent $C$ such that, for every $n\ge0$ and every field $V$ with $\langle V\rangle_s=0$ and $\kappa\cdot V=0$, $$\begin{equation}
 \|C[V]\|_{[n],\gamma}\le C_n\mathsf D^C\lambda^{-1}\|V\|_{[n+1],\gamma}. \tag{5.31}
\end{equation}$$ If moreover $V_t+MV+\kappa\partial_s\mathsf p=f$ with $\langle f\rangle_s=0$ and $\langle \mathsf p\rangle_s=0$, then $$\begin{equation}
 \|(\partial_t+M)C[V]+\mathbf d\mathsf p\|_{[n],\gamma}
 \le C_n\mathsf D^C\lambda^{-1}
             \bigl(\|V\|_{[n+1],\gamma}+\|f\|_{[n+1],\gamma}\bigr). \tag{5.32}
\end{equation}$$ For the leading oscillation $V=A_1$ on $[t_0,T]$, the source in this estimate is $f=0$.", "status": "unverified"}

{"assumption_ids": [], "boundary": "A result as the authors number it. Its links to the Lean were made with AI for Proof Reader and have not been reviewed by the authors.", "citation_ids": ["cite.euler.lemma-5.7"], "evidence_ids": ["link.euler.lemma-5.7"], "id": "euler.lemma-5.7", "kind": "claim", "label": "Lemma 5.7", "locator": "Lemma 5.7, Section 5", "source_id": "paper", "statement": "**Lemma 5.7.** Let $g_0(t,a,s)$ be smooth and independent of the phase at the labels outside $\operatorname{supp}g$. Assume its phase average extends smoothly and periodically in $a$ across the cell faces. There is an absolute exponent $C$ such that, for every integer $m\ge0$ and every $t\in[t_0,T]$, $$\begin{align}
 \sum_{|\beta|=m}\|\partial_x^\beta g_0^\natural(t)\|_{L^\infty}
 &\le C_m(\mathsf D^C\lambda^{3/2})^m\gamma(t)
                  \|g_0\|_{[m],\gamma}, \tag{5.44}\\
 \|g_0^\natural(t)\|_{C^{m,1/2}}
 &\le C_m(\mathsf D^C\lambda^{3/2})^{m+1}\gamma(t)
                  \|g_0\|_{[m+1],\gamma}. \tag{5.45}
\end{align}$$ Both bounds also hold on $[0,T]$ with $\gamma$ replaced by one, and for a phase-independent field with $\mathsf D^C\lambda^{3/2}$ replaced by $\mathsf D^C$.", "status": "unverified"}

{"assumption_ids": [], "boundary": "A result as the authors number it. Its links to the Lean were made with AI for Proof Reader and have not been reviewed by the authors.", "citation_ids": ["cite.euler.lemma-5.8"], "evidence_ids": ["link.euler.lemma-5.8"], "id": "euler.lemma-5.8", "kind": "claim", "label": "Lemma 5.8", "locator": "Lemma 5.8, Section 5", "source_id": "paper", "statement": "**Lemma 5.8.** The field $V$ is smooth, odd and divergence free, $\Pi$ is smooth, and $$\begin{equation}
 V_t+V\cdot\nabla V+\nabla\Pi=R_V,\qquad
 R_V=R_P^\natural+R_{\widetilde\chi}^\natural . \tag{5.46}
\end{equation}$$ The oscillation satisfies $$\begin{equation}
 \|W_{\rm osc}^\natural\|_{C_tL^\infty}
 \le C_{0,P}\mathsf D^b\lambda^{-5/4}, \tag{5.47}
\end{equation}$$ and for every $m\ge0$, $$\begin{equation}
\begin{gathered}
 \|W_{\rm osc}^\natural\|_{C_tC^{m,1/2}}
 \le C_{m,P}\mathsf D^{b(m+2)}\lambda^{3(m+1)/2-5/4},\qquad
 \|\bar W^\natural\|_{C_tC^{m,1/2}}
 \le C_{m,P}\mathsf D^{b(m+2)}\lambda^{-5/2},\\
 \|R_V\|_{C_tC^{1,1/2}}
 \le C_{\rm step}\lambda^2
   \bigl(\lambda^{-2P/3}+\|\widetilde\chi'\gamma\|_\infty\bigr),
\end{gathered} \tag{5.48}
\end{equation}$$ where the last supremum is over $[t_0,T]$.", "status": "unverified"}

{"assumption_ids": [], "boundary": "A result as the authors number it. Its links to the Lean were made with AI for Proof Reader and have not been reviewed by the authors.", "citation_ids": ["cite.euler.lemma-5.9"], "evidence_ids": ["link.euler.lemma-5.9"], "id": "euler.lemma-5.9", "kind": "claim", "label": "Lemma 5.9", "locator": "Lemma 5.9, Section 5", "source_id": "paper", "statement": "**Lemma 5.9.** On $[0,T]\times\mathbb T^3$, $$\begin{equation}
 D_x^2\mathsf P^\natural\le C_{\rm step}\lambda^{-1/4}\operatorname{Id}. \tag{5.49}
\end{equation}$$ At $x=0$, for $t_0\le t\le T$, $$\begin{equation}
 \nabla V(t,0)=\nabla U(t,0)
   +\widetilde\chi(t)\,\mathbf a(t,0)\otimes\kappa(t,0)+E_c(t),
 \qquad
 |E_c|\le\lambda^{-1/3} . \tag{5.50}
\end{equation}$$", "status": "unverified"}

{"assumption_ids": [], "boundary": "A result as the authors number it. Its links to the Lean were made with AI for Proof Reader and have not been reviewed by the authors.", "citation_ids": ["cite.euler.lemma-a.2"], "evidence_ids": ["link.euler.lemma-a.2"], "id": "euler.lemma-a.2", "kind": "claim", "label": "Lemma A.2", "locator": "Lemma A.2, Section A", "source_id": "paper", "statement": "**Lemma A.2.** Let either $H=\mathbb R^3$, $\mathcal R=\mathbb P$, or $H=L^2(I;\mathbb R^3)$, $\mathcal R=\mathbb P\mathcal Q$, where $\mathcal Qv=v-\int_0^1v(t)\,\mathrm dt$. Let $B\in C^{1,1/2}(\mathbb T^3;\mathcal L(H))$ and $\mu>0$. Suppose $$\begin{equation}
 \langle B(a)w,w\rangle_H\ge\mu\left\lVert w\right\rVert_H^2 \tag{A.4}
\end{equation}$$ for every $a\in\mathbb T^3$ and every $w\in H$, with $\mathcal Qw=w$ in the second case. Then $\mathcal RB$ is an isomorphism on $\mathcal RC^{0,1/2}(\mathbb T^3;H)$, and $$\begin{equation}
 \left\lVert v\right\rVert_{C^{0,1/2}_a(H)}
 \le C(2+\mu^{-1}+\left\lVert B\right\rVert_{C^1_a\mathcal L(H)})^{p}
            \left\lVert\mathcal RBv\right\rVert_{C^{0,1/2}_a(H)}. \tag{A.5}
\end{equation}$$ If moreover $B\in C^{k,1/2}(\mathbb T^3;\mathcal L(H))$ for an integer $k\ge1$, the same operator is an isomorphism on $\mathcal RC^{k,1/2}(\mathbb T^3;H)$.", "status": "unverified"}

{"assumption_ids": [], "boundary": "A result as the authors number it. Its links to the Lean were made with AI for Proof Reader and have not been reviewed by the authors.", "citation_ids": ["cite.euler.lemma-b.1"], "evidence_ids": ["link.euler.lemma-b.1"], "id": "euler.lemma-b.1", "kind": "claim", "label": "Lemma B.1", "locator": "Lemma B.1, Section B", "source_id": "paper", "statement": "**Lemma B.1.** $S_\Lambda$ commutes with derivatives, preserves parity and divergence freeness, and maps into fields supported on frequencies at most $2\Lambda$. Its operator norms on $L^\infty$ and on $C^{0,1/2}$ are bounded in terms of $\psi$ alone. For every integer $m\ge0$ and every normed-space-valued $f$ supported on frequencies at most $4\Lambda$, $$\begin{equation}
 \sum_{|\beta|=m}\left\lVert\partial^\beta f\right\rVert_\infty\le C_m\Lambda^m\left\lVert f\right\rVert_\infty . \tag{B.1}
\end{equation}$$ For all integers $0\le s\le M$ and every $f\in C^{M,1/2}$, $$\begin{equation}
 \left\lVert(\operatorname{Id}-S_\Lambda)f\right\rVert_{C^{s,1/2}}
 \le C_M\Lambda^{s-M}\left\lVert f\right\rVert_{C^{M,1/2}}. \tag{B.2}
\end{equation}$$ The constants $C_m$ and $C_M$ depend only on their index and on $\psi$.", "status": "unverified"}

{"assumption_ids": [], "boundary": "A result as the authors number it. Its links to the Lean were made with AI for Proof Reader and have not been reviewed by the authors.", "citation_ids": ["cite.euler.lemma-d.1"], "evidence_ids": ["link.euler.lemma-d.1"], "id": "euler.lemma-d.1", "kind": "claim", "label": "Lemma D.1", "locator": "Lemma D.1, Section D", "source_id": "paper", "statement": "**Lemma D.1.** For every $n\ge0$ and fields $f,g$, $$\begin{align}
 \sum_i\|\partial_{a_i}f\|_{[n],\gamma}
 &\le C\ell_*^{-1}\|f\|_{[n+1],\gamma},\qquad
 \|\partial_sf\|_{[n],\gamma}
 \le h^{-1}\|f\|_{[n+1],\gamma}, \tag{D.3}\\
 \|fg\|_{[n],\gamma}
 &\le C_n\|f\|_{[n]}\|g\|_{[n],\gamma}. \tag{D.4}
\end{align}$$ With $\langle f\rangle_s$ the phase average, $$\begin{equation}
 \|\langle f\rangle_s\|_{[n],\gamma}\le\|f\|_{[n],\gamma},\qquad
 \|f-\langle f\rangle_s\|_{[n],\gamma}\le2\|f\|_{[n],\gamma},\qquad
 \|\partial_s^{-1}f\|_{[n],\gamma}\le C\|f\|_{[n],\gamma}
 \quad(\langle f\rangle_s=0). \tag{D.5}
\end{equation}$$ For phase-independent fields, (D.4) also holds with $\|\cdot\|_{[n],1/2}$ in place of $\|\cdot\|_{[n]}$ and $\gamma\equiv1$.", "status": "unverified"}

{"assumption_ids": [], "boundary": "A result as the authors number it. Its links to the Lean were made with AI for Proof Reader and have not been reviewed by the authors.", "citation_ids": ["cite.euler.proposition-2.8"], "evidence_ids": ["link.euler.proposition-2.8"], "id": "euler.proposition-2.8", "kind": "claim", "label": "Proposition 2.8", "locator": "Proposition 2.8, Section 2", "source_id": "paper", "statement": "**Proposition 2.8.** Assume (2.7), let $\mathbf a$ be the amplitude vector of Lemma 2.6, with the couplings of Lemma 2.7, and suppose that $0<\varepsilon_+\le\varepsilon/32$ and $T\ge2(\varepsilon\varepsilon_+)^{-1/2}$. Then the interval $\tau_m\le\tau\le2(\varepsilon\varepsilon_+)^{-1/2}$, where $\tau_m\ge2/\varepsilon$ is the zero of $Y$ (Lemma 2.5), contains a crossing. At every crossing in this interval the strength $\sigma_+=|\mathbf a(\tau)||k(\tau)|$ satisfies $$\begin{equation}
 C^{-1}(\varepsilon\varepsilon_+)^{-1/2}
 \le\frac{\sigma_+}{\lambda\max_{[0,T]}|\mathbf a|}
 \le C(\varepsilon\varepsilon_+)^{-1/2}. \tag{2.23}
\end{equation}$$ Moreover $$\begin{equation}
 |\mathbf a(\tau)|\le\exp\bigl(3-\varepsilon^{-1/2}\bigr)
 \max_{[0,T]}|\mathbf a|\qquad(0\le\tau\le1). \tag{2.24}
\end{equation}$$", "status": "unverified"}

{"assumption_ids": [], "boundary": "A result as the authors number it. Its links to the Lean were made with AI for Proof Reader and have not been reviewed by the authors.", "citation_ids": ["cite.euler.proposition-5.1"], "evidence_ids": ["link.euler.proposition-5.1"], "id": "euler.proposition-5.1", "kind": "claim", "label": "Proposition 5.1", "locator": "Proposition 5.1, Section 5", "source_id": "paper", "statement": "**Proposition 5.1.** There is an absolute exponent $C$ such that each coefficient $c$ among $$(D_aX)^{\pm1},\qquad M,\qquad
 \kappa/\lambda,\qquad \lambda^2/|\kappa|^2,\qquad P_\kappa$$ satisfies (D.6). Let $f(t,x)$ be a field with values in a normed space such that $|f|_m\le C_m'A_f\mathsf D^m$ for every $m\ge0$ and a number $A_f$. Then $$\begin{equation}
 |f\circ X|_m\le C_mA_f\mathsf D^{Cm}\qquad(m\ge0). \tag{5.7}
\end{equation}$$ Consequently, for every $n\ge0$, $$\begin{equation}
 \|c\|_{[n]}\le C_n\mathsf D^C,\qquad
 \|f\circ X\|_{[n]}\le C_nA_f . \tag{5.8}
\end{equation}$$", "status": "unverified"}

{"assumption_ids": [], "boundary": "A result as the authors number it. Its links to the Lean were made with AI for Proof Reader and have not been reviewed by the authors.", "citation_ids": ["cite.euler.proposition-5.11"], "evidence_ids": ["link.euler.proposition-5.11"], "id": "euler.proposition-5.11", "kind": "claim", "label": "Proposition 5.11", "locator": "Proposition 5.11, Section 5", "source_id": "paper", "statement": "**Proposition 5.11.** Let $\Lambda\ge1$, and with the depth $P$ of the expansion put $$\begin{equation}
 u=S_\Lambda V,\qquad v_\Lambda=u-V,\qquad
 \varepsilon_\Lambda=\Lambda^{2-P}
 \bigl(1+\left\lVert V\right\rVert_{C_tC^{P,1/2}}\bigr)^2 . \tag{5.55}
\end{equation}$$ Let $p$ be the function of zero mean with $\Delta p=-\operatorname{tr}((\nabla u)^2)$, and let $R_u=u_t+u\cdot\nabla u+\nabla p$. Then $u$ is smooth, odd, divergence free and supported on frequencies at most $2\Lambda$, its residual $R_u$ is odd, divergence free and supported on frequencies at most $4\Lambda$, and $$\begin{equation}
 \left\lVert v_\Lambda\right\rVert_{C_tC^{2,1/2}}\le C_{2,P}\varepsilon_\Lambda,\qquad
 \left\lVert R_u\right\rVert_{C_tC^{1,1/2}}+\left\lVert D^2(p-\Pi)\right\rVert_{C_tC^{0,1/2}}
 \le C\left\lVert R_V\right\rVert_{C_tC^{1,1/2}}+C_{2,P}\varepsilon_\Lambda . \tag{5.56}
\end{equation}$$ Moreover, on $[0,T]\times\mathbb T^3$, $$\begin{equation}
 D^2(p-p_U)\le\bigl(C_{\rm step}\lambda^{-1/4}
   +C\left\lVert R_V\right\rVert_{C_tC^{1,1/2}}+C_{2,P}\varepsilon_\Lambda\bigr)\operatorname{Id}. \tag{5.57}
\end{equation}$$", "status": "unverified"}

