### 1.1 Waves and successive amplification

We first describe the wave construction of Craik and Criminale \[[12](#cite-CraikCriminale)\]. Let $D(t)$ be a smooth matrix with $\operatorname{tr}D=0$ and $D'+D^2$ symmetric. These conditions ensure that $U(t,x)=D(t)x$, linear in space, solves the Euler equations on $\mathbb R^3$, with pressure $p_D(t,x)=-x\cdot(D'+D^2)x/2$. We add to this linear background a velocity $$w(t,x)=\mathbf a(t)F(k(t)\cdot x),\qquad \mathbf a\cdot k=0,$$ where $k(t)\ne0$ is the wave vector, $\mathbf a(t)$ is the amplitude vector, and $F$ is a smooth odd $1$-periodic profile of the scalar phase $s$. For example, one may take $F(s)=\sin(2\pi s)$. The vector $\mathbf a$ records both size and direction: the velocity amplitude is $|\mathbf a|\|F\|_\infty$, and $\mathbf a/|\mathbf a|$ is its unit direction when $\mathbf a\ne0$. The unit wave vector is $k/|k|$. Since $F$ has period one, $w$ is periodic in the direction of $k$ with period $1/|k|$, so $|k|$ is the spatial frequency of the wave.

The sum $u=Dx+w$ is an exact solution of the Euler equations if $k$ and $\mathbf a$ solve $$\begin{equation}
 k'=-D^Tk,\qquad
 \mathbf a'=-D\mathbf a+p_{\rm w} k,\qquad
 p_{\rm w}=\frac{2k\cdot D\mathbf a}{|k|^2}. \tag{1.4}
\end{equation}$$ Its pressure is $p=p_D-p_{\rm w} P(k\cdot x)$, where $P'=F$. Indeed, $\mathbf a\cdot k=0$ gives $(w\cdot\nabla)w=0$, the first ordinary differential equation transports the phase by $Dx$, and the second cancels the remaining acceleration with the gradient of the wave pressure $-p_{\rm w}P(k\cdot x)$. The displayed value of $p_{\rm w}$ preserves $\mathbf a\cdot k=0$. We call (1.4) the Craik–Criminale equations for the wave vector and the amplitude vector. Normalizing $F'(0)=1$ gives $\nabla w(t,0)=\mathbf a\otimes k$. Related short-wave instabilities of planar vortices were studied by Pierrehumbert \[[35](#cite-Pierrehumbert1986)\] and Bayly \[[36](#cite-Bayly1986)\].

To describe amplification of a new wave, write $k_p$ and $\mathbf a_p$ for the nonzero wave vector and amplitude vector of the preceding wave, and set $$E_1=\frac{k_p}{|k_p|},\qquad
 E_2=\frac{\mathbf a_p}{|\mathbf a_p|},\qquad E_3=E_1\times E_2,
 \qquad \sigma=|\mathbf a_p|\,|k_p|.$$ $E_1$ is the unit wave vector and $E_2$ the direction vector of the preceding wave. The preceding wave contributes the velocity gradient $\mathbf a_p\otimes k_p=\sigma E_2\otimes E_1$ at the origin. Here $(E_2\otimes E_1)v=(E_1\cdot v)E_2$: the corresponding linear velocity is $\sigma(E_1\cdot x)E_2$, a shear in direction $E_2$ varying in direction $E_1$. The new wave therefore sees $D=B+\sigma E_2\otimes E_1$, where $B$ is the older background gradient. To model the wave equations at the origin, take $B$ with just two nonzero entries in this moving frame, both constant, $$E_1\cdot BE_2=\nu>0,\qquad E_3\cdot BE_2=\eta>0,
 \qquad \varepsilon=\eta/\nu\ll1.$$ Start the new wave at time $t=0$ with $k=\lambda E_3$, where $\lambda>0$ is its initial frequency, and write $a_i=\mathbf a\cdot E_i$. The preceding wave evolves by (1.4) with $D=B$. Differentiating its normalized vectors gives, in this case, $E_1'=-\nu E_2$, $E_2'=\nu E_1-\eta E_3$ and $E_3'=\eta E_2$. Combining these frame equations with (1.4) for the new wave gives, at $t=0$, $$\dot a_1=-2\nu a_2,\qquad
 \dot a_2=-(\sigma-\nu)a_1.$$ Freezing these coefficients gives a growing mode with rate $\sqrt{2\nu(\sigma-\nu)}$ when $\sigma>\nu$. We choose the initial direction of the amplitude along $E_2-\sqrt{2\nu/\sigma}\,E_1$, the growing direction to leading order for $\sigma\gg\nu$.

The coefficients evolve as the wave vector turns. Let $\varphi$ be the angle of $k$ from $E_3$ toward $E_1$: $$\tan\varphi=\frac{k\cdot E_1}{k\cdot E_3},$$ so $\varphi=0$ at $t=0$. The coupling $\eta$ tilts $k$ toward $-E_2$, and the shear carries this tilt into $E_1$, so $\varphi$ increases; while $\eta t$ is small, $\tan\varphi$ grows like $(\sigma+\nu)\eta t^2$. In the strong-shear limit $\sigma/\nu\to\infty$, the wave pressure multiplies the driving coefficient $2\nu$ in the equation for $a_1$ by $\cos^2\varphi\,(1-\varepsilon\tan\varphi)$ (Lemma 2.3). This factor changes sign at $\tan\varphi=1/\varepsilon$, and in the strong-shear limit the amplitude $|\mathbf a|$ reaches its peak after this sign change, as shown in Figure 1. For the perturbed wave with this initial direction, Proposition 2.8 gives a *gain* of at least a fixed multiple of $\exp(\varepsilon^{-1/2})$ on its prescribed interval. The gain is the ratio of the maximum of $|\mathbf a|$ on that interval to its initial value.

![Figure 1](figures/figure-1.svg)

**Figure 1.** Turning and growth in the case of two nonzero entries, in the limit of a strong shear. Left: the angle $\varphi$ of the wave vector from $E_3$ toward $E_1$. Right: amplitude as a fraction of its peak. The dashed line marks the amplitude peak, by which time $\varphi$ is close to $\pi/2$.

Size alone does not determine the successor wave. The new wave must supply both a large shear and a suitable frame for the next step of the iteration. In its frame (the subscript $+$ denotes the frame of the newly added wave), $$E_{1,+}=k/|k|,\qquad E_{2,+}=\mathbf a/|\mathbf a|,\qquad
 E_{3,+}=E_{1,+}\times E_{2,+},$$ its shear is $\sigma_+E_{2,+}\otimes E_{1,+}$, with $\sigma_+=|\mathbf a|\,|k|$. The older gradient $D$, expressed in this new frame, must again have positive couplings $$\nu_+=E_{1,+}\cdot D E_{2,+},\qquad
 \eta_+=E_{3,+}\cdot D E_{2,+}.$$ These play the roles of $\nu$ and $\eta$ in the next amplification step. We prescribe a smaller ratio $\eta_+/\nu_+=\varepsilon_+<\varepsilon$ to give the next wave enough amplification to keep the errors small. The turning of the wave vector supplies this orientation. In the strong-shear limit, $\nu_+$ starts at $\varepsilon\nu$, and $\eta_+$ stays within a factor $1+O(\varepsilon)$ of $\nu\cos\varphi$; at late times this results from a cancellation between terms much larger than $\nu\cos\varphi$. For the perturbed wave, Lemma 2.7 shows that at late times $\nu_+$ is close to $\nu$ and $\eta_+/\nu_+$ is comparable to $\cos\varphi$. Under the hypotheses of Proposition 2.8, the ratio reaches the prescribed value after the amplitude peaks in the strong-shear limit, while the new shear remains large. We start the successor when the ratio reaches that threshold. Thus the Craik–Criminale equations produce both amplification and the orientation needed to repeat the construction.

The periodic construction starts from the stationary shear flow $$U_0(x)=\sin(2\pi x_2)(e_1+\rho e_3),$$ where $e_i$ are the coordinate vectors and $\rho>0$ is small. It solves the Euler equations with zero pressure. In the initial coordinate frame its central gradient has $\nu=2\pi$ and $\eta=2\pi\rho$, so that $\varepsilon=\rho$. Its explicit flow supplies the first wave; subsequent waves use the successive gradients they create.

Craik–Criminale waves on $\mathbb R^3$ provide a local model for amplification. For the periodic construction, we localize each wave by a smooth cutoff supported in the interior of a fundamental cell in Lagrangian coordinates. We extend the localized field periodically and transport its phase and cutoff by the preceding periodic velocity, as in geometric optics \[[37](#cite-LifschitzHameiri1991), [38](#cite-FriedlanderVishik1991), [39](#cite-Lax1957), [40](#cite-ChoquetBruhat1969)\]. Since the cutoff vanishes near the cell boundary, the wave vector need not lie in $\mathbb Z^3$. Localization and the spatial variation of the background introduce errors, which the corrections below make small at each step and remove in the limit.

